% ============================================================================
% congress-chamber — the two chambers the U.S. Congress is divided into
% (adj-facts-stdlib, CIVICS).
% ============================================================================
% This file exists to CLOSE an abstention its own sibling deliberately
% opened. `government-branch-member.adj` ships
% `government_branch_member(legislative, congress)` and abstains on
% `senate`/`house_of_representatives`, because USA.gov's "Branches of the
% U.S. government" page nests those two UNDER Congress in a
% colon-introduced sub-list rather than naming them as direct members of
% the branch. That sibling's header names THIS table as the intended home
% for them; here it is. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? congress_chamber(senate, $P)
%       % congress
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `congress_chamber(chamber, parent)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the parent body WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on any body that is not one of these two
% source-cited chambers.
%
% The relation is MULTI-VALUED on `parent`, which is the direction that
% answers the question a learner is actually asked — "what are the two
% chambers of Congress?" is one query returning two solutions:
%
%     ? congress_chamber($C, congress)
%       % senate
%       % house_of_representatives
%
% WHY A SEPARATE TABLE, not two more rows on `government_branch_member`:
% the sub-list states a DIFFERENT relation from the one that table holds.
% `government_branch_member` answers "who makes up this BRANCH"; this
% answers "what are the chambers of this BODY". Flattening the chambers
% into branch members would have silently discarded one level of the
% nesting the source itself states. Keeping them apart means the two
% compose instead: `congress_chamber(senate, congress)` and
% `government_branch_member(legislative, congress)` together ground "the
% Senate is part of the legislative branch" as a two-hop DERIVATION whose
% provenance is the composition of its premises, rather than a third
% asserted row nobody can audit back to a sentence. (That derived
% `rule` is a natural next slice; it is deliberately NOT part of this one.)
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from USA.gov's "Branches of the U.S.
% government" page. The page's own markup is a sentence stem followed by a
% two-item list, and this table decodes exactly that structure:
%
%     source markup (verbatim)                              this table's row
%     ----------------------------------------------------  ------------------------------------------
%     "The legislative branch is made up of Congress:"       (the stem naming the parent body)
%     "The U.S. Senate"                                      (senate, congress)
%     "The U.S. House of Representatives"                    (house_of_representatives, congress)
%
% curl-fetched the raw page and read the markup byte-for-byte before
% writing this file (not just an AI-summarized WebFetch) — confirmed the
% stem is a single `<p>` reading "The legislative branch is made up of
% Congress:" (the linked anchor text inlined) and that the two chambers
% are its immediately-following `<ul>`'s only two `<li>` items, with no
% third. `trust authoritative` — USA.gov is the U.S. General Services
% Administration's official guide to government information, a
% first-party .gov publisher of exactly the fact being recalled, the same
% tier and the SAME page `government-branch-member.adj` already cites.
%
% Honest abstention. The list has exactly two items, so the two-chamber
% domain is covered with no gaps — but a body that is NOT one of them
% ABSTAINS rather than being invented into a chamber:
%
%   - `supreme_court` and `president` are genuinely named institutions of
%     the U.S. federal government, and the SAME page names both — but of
%     the judicial and executive branches, not as chambers of Congress.
%     They are already correctly tabled in `government-branch-member.adj`
%     against their own branches.
%   - `special agencies and offices that provide support services to
%     Congress` — the open-ended category phrase the same paragraph
%     attaches to the legislative branch — names no specific body, and is
%     not part of the chamber sub-list at all.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table congress_chamber {
    columns chamber, parent

    row (senate, congress)
    row (house_of_representatives, congress)

    source "The legislative branch is made up of Congress:"
    locator "https://www.usa.gov/branches-of-government"
    trust authoritative
}
